        "source": "pdf_layout",
        "field": "abstract",
        "raw_text": "Protocols for secure Multi-Party Computation (MPC) can be classified according to the underlying communication model. Two promi- nent communication models considered in the literature are the syn- chronous and asynchronous models, which considerably differ in terms of the achievable security guarantees. Synchronous MPC protocols can achieve the optimal corruption thresholdn/2 and allow every party to give input, but become completely insecure when synchrony assumptions are violated. On the other hand, asynchronous MPC protocols remain secure under arbitrary network conditions, but can tolerate onlyn/3 corruptions and parties with slow connections unavoidably cannot give input. A natural question is whether there exists a protocol for MPC that can tolerate up tot s <n /2 corruptions under a synchronous network and ta <n /3 corruptions even when the network is asynchronous. We answer this question by showing tight feasibility and impossibility results. More specifically, we show that such a protocol exists if and only ifta +2ts <n and the number of inputs taken into account under an asynchronous net- work is at most n − ts.",
